{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition starts the verification test workflow — for example, is it triggered by a fixed schedule, a request from another team, or a request made by the engineer?",
      "answer": "The workflow begins when a formal test request arrives. The engineer first looks over that incoming request along with any associated conditions before doing anything else. The origin of who sends the request is not specified beyond it being an input that kicks off the process."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What are the distinct steps the engineer performs when preparing the verification test, and are any of those steps performed in an external tool or system?",
      "answer": "The engineer reads through the request and its conditions, then configures the evaluation environment based on a setup guide. After that, the test is executed using a dedicated test-execution tool. So yes, at least the execution step involves an external tool. Specific configuration substeps beyond this are not detailed."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What specific criteria or thresholds does the engineer use to decide that a result 'looks wrong' and requires investigation?",
      "answer": "Two checks are made: first, whether the output files are all present and can be read without errors; second, whether the data inside those files shows unusual or unexpected patterns. If files are missing, corrupted, or the patterns look abnormal, that signals something is wrong. Exact numeric thresholds are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "When an investigation is triggered, what steps does it involve, and can it result in re-running the test or does it always end with sharing findings?",
      "answer": "Abnormal or incomplete results are listed as exceptions in the process, but explicit investigation steps beyond flagging them are not detailed. A re-run path is not described. The workflow moves toward writing a summary and distributing it regardless. Whether a re-run is possible is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Who are the 'related people' that receive the shared result, and through what channel is that result communicated to them?",
      "answer": "The completed write-up is passed to a colleague acting as a reviewing engineer, who may also loop in a project owner. Communication happens through an email or collaborative messaging platform. The reviewing engineer can ask for clarification if anything in the document is unclear."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What information must the short report contain, and where is it submitted or stored once it is written?",
      "answer": "The document captures a summary of what the engineer found during the evaluation. It is drafted using a pre-existing template and then sent out for peer review. A storage location such as a central repository is mentioned as part of the surrounding systems, but where the final report is formally archived is not explicitly stated."
    }
  ]
}
